          "icon": "layers"
        },
        {
          "label": "Mantle Depth",
          "value": "2,900 km",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Core Composition",
          "value": "NIFE (Ni + Fe)",
          "icon": "award"
        }
      ],
      "id": "p6f3jzh"
    },
    {
      "id": "u4989yx",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Radius and General Facts about the Earth"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Earth consists of three principal concentric layers: Crust, Mantle, and Core.",
        "The Crust is subdivided into Continental Crust (Sial: Silica and Aluminium) and Oceanic Crust (Sima: Silica and Magnesium).",
        "The Mantle reaches a depth of 2,900 km and includes the semi-fluid Asthenosphere responsible for tectonic plate motion.",
        "The Core is divided into a liquid Outer Core (generating Earth's magnetic field) and a solid Inner Core.",
        "Internal boundaries are defined by seismic discontinuities including Conrad, Mohorovičić (Moho), Repetti, Gutenberg, and Lehmann."
      ],
      "id": "8l4iu12"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ltr2dk6",
      "content": "• Average radius of Earth: about 6371 km."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7d6m4dg",
      "content": "• Earth's interior temperature increases with depth (geothermal gradient)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "n5f6ug8",
      "content": "• Density also increases toward the center."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9ijz356",
      "content": "• The Earth is composed mainly of iron, oxygen, silicon, magnesium and nickel."
    },
    {
      "id": "6io6dsw",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Sources of Information about the Earth's Interior"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "8yid340",
      "content": "A. Direct Sources"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "absxjml",
      "content": "• Mining and deep drilling projects."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mihc2kz",
      "content": "• Volcanic eruptions bring materials from inside the Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "fqe0t9b",
      "content": "B. Indirect Sources"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "n9xci51",
      "content": "• Seismic waves produced by earthquakes."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "oksm0ao",
      "content": "• Gravitational force studies."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "i0unr4x",
      "content": "• Magnetic field observations."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4asrwui",
      "content": "• Meteorite composition which is similar to Earth's interior."
    },
    {
      "id": "03uj5cp",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Major Layers of the Earth"
    },
    {
