          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "y34ihc5",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Crust"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ptwmrpm",
      "content": "• The crust is the outermost layer of the Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nu5ad1h",
      "content": "• It is very thin compared to other layers."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7gchomm",
      "content": "• Average thickness is about 35 km under continents."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rn5f3wr",
      "content": "• Oceanic crust is thinner (5–10 km)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gmpjuuw",
      "content": "• Main elements are oxygen, silicon, aluminum, iron and calcium."
    },
    {
      "id": "apkolrd",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Types of Crust"
    },
    {
      "id": "cs2usb7",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Thickness</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Composition</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Continental Crust</p>",
            "<p>30–70 km</p>",
            "<p>Granite rocks rich in silica and alumina</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Oceanic Crust</p>",
            "<p>5–10 km</p>",
            "<p>Basalt rocks rich in silica and magnesium</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "264itgr",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Mantle"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bltrye0",
      "content": "• The mantle lies below the crust and extends up to about 2900 km depth."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1px5z4y",
      "content": "• It forms nearly 84% of Earth's volume."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Continental Crust (Sial)",
        "Oceanic Crust (Sima)"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Primary Composition",
          "Silica and Aluminium",
          "Silica and Magnesium"
        ],
        [
          "Average Thickness",
          "30 – 70 km (Thicker)",
          "5 – 10 km (Thinner)"
        ],
        [
          "Density",
          "Lower (~2.7 g/cm³)",
          "Higher (~3.0 g/cm³)"
        ],
        [
          "Dominant Rock Type",
          "Granitic / Felsic",
          "Basaltic / Mafic"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "68bi0dx"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rjz5itv",
      "content": "• The upper part of the mantle with the crust forms the lithosphere."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "onsslim",
      "content": "• Below it lies the asthenosphere which contains partially molten rocks."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k47rzgj",
      "content": "• The mantle plays a major role in plate tectonic movements."
    },
    {
      "id": "66pmyc4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Important Zones in the Mantle"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vmpcj21",
      "content": "• Lithosphere – rigid outer layer including crust and upper mantle."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ma23uwn",
      "content": "• Asthenosphere – semi-molten layer where tectonic plates float."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "owh45yu",
      "content": "• Mesosphere – lower mantle with higher density."
    },
    {
      "id": "bcty3mt",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Core"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "z0yolsl",
      "content": "• The core is the innermost part of the Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gpac3c5",
      "content": "• It extends from 2900 km to the center at 6371 km."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "trc7og5",
      "content": "• It is mainly composed of iron and nickel."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7qlcjhz",
      "content": "• The core is responsible for generating Earth's magnetic field."
    },
    {
      "id": "bx7ycbg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Types of Core"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Moho Discontinuity lies between the Crust and Mantle, while Gutenberg Discontinuity separates the Mantle from the Core (~2,900 km depth).",
        "Sial forms the lighter continental crust, whereas Sima forms the denser oceanic crust.",
        "The Outer Core remains in a liquid state while the Inner Core is solid due to extreme pressure.",
        "Seismic S-waves cannot travel through the liquid outer core, producing an S-wave shadow zone beyond 103°."
      ],
      "id": "242hx8t"
    },
